<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abu Yaqub Yusuf I</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abu Yaqub Yusuf I was the second <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate</a>, son and successor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-mumin">Abd al-Mu'min</a>. He consolidated Almohad rule in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> and was a patron of learning, supporting philosophers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-tufayl">Ibn Tufayl</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/averroes">Ibn Rushd</a> (Averroes). He also sponsored building works in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>, and he died in 1184 during a campaign in Portugal.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abu-yaqub-yusuf-i.jpg" alt="Abu Yaqub Yusuf I" width="320" height="159" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MoneyMuseum · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abu_Yaqub_Yusef_Coin.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
